# value_struct_mut.tkr — the item-14 (§13) ACCEPT channel for the MUTABLE value struct. The item
# removes the "struct is readonly" block so a struct mutates IN PLACE like a class WITHOUT becoming an
# object: it stays a VALUE (copied on assignment/pass, no identity, no arena). That is TWO obligations,
# not one, and these rows pin both — the mutation must STICK (mutation_sticks, encapsulated_setter) and
# it must NOT leak across a copy (copy_is_independent). The mechanism is §13.2: every value struct
# carries a fat header (a self-pointer word) and a method takes its receiver as that pointer, so `self`
# is a ref and a write lands on the caller's own materialization.
#
# `mutation_sticks` is the anti-regression that names the whole item: before the fat-header/receiver-ref
# work it returned 0, because the receiver was a by-value copy and every write died with it. The REJECT
# twin — readonly enforcement and mutating-method refusals — is
# `examples/regressions/value_struct_mut_reject`.

Feature: value_struct_mut — item-14 mutable value struct (self as ref, value semantics preserved)

  Scenario: mutation_sticks — two `bump`s on one materialization both land; `self.n = …` STICKS (→ 2, was 0 pre-item-14)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario mutation_sticks: ok"

  Scenario: copy_is_independent — `b = a` is a fresh materialization, so mutating the copy leaves the original alone (→ 56: a=5, b=6)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario copy_is_independent: ok"

  Scenario: encapsulated_setter — a private `_v` written only through the type's own `set_v` (the Intent `pub set self._x` case) (→ 7)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario encapsulated_setter: ok"

  Scenario: ref_reassigns_slot — a `ref` parameter aliases the caller's slot, so assigning a whole new struct through it replaces the caller's value (→ 90: 9 before, 0 after)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario ref_reassigns_slot: ok"
